<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  HeliSim's control map: which physical control flies which.

  This is the project's own default, and it is the mapping the sandbox has
  always been flown from: W and S for the collective, the up and down arrows for
  the longitudinal cyclic, left and right for the lateral one, and A and D for
  the pedals.  main.py reads it unless the command line names another file, and
  controls.py's own self test flies it.

  Every control may be moved to another device on its own, so a home made panel
  can arrive one axis at a time: name a <device> and point a <control> at it,
  and leave the rest as they are.  A control this file does not mention keeps
  the keyboard mapping above.

  An Arduino Leonardo sent as a generic USB HID game controller reports a name
  beginning "Arduino", so its device is found by match rather than by index: an
  index moves the day another controller is plugged in.  To fly the cyclic and
  the pedals from one, add its device and swap those four controls onto it:

      <device name="huey" kind="joystick" match="Arduino" />
      <control name="cyclic-long" source="huey" axis="1" deadzone="0.03" />
      <control name="cyclic-lat"  source="huey" axis="0" deadzone="0.03" />
      <control name="pedals"      source="huey" axis="3" deadzone="0.05" />

  axis is the joystick's own axis number, counting from zero in the order the
  device reports them.  invert="true" turns a sense over, deadzone is how far
  from its rest the device has to read before it is believed, and minimum and
  maximum calibrate a lever that does not reach the ends of its own range.

  The throttle is deliberately absent: the map this project ships names it while
  there is nothing on the other end of it.  The model's own aircraft has no engine
  - the report's fixed rotor, which is what TM-73254's figures were computed with
  - so a run begins without one and `main.py`'s `F` key fits a T53 to the shaft in
  flight (see simulation.Simulation.fit_engine).  The keyboard's `Q` and `E` roll
  the grip today; the control to name here when a joystick or a home made
  collective has one is "throttle", and that is the next thing the device map
  wants.
-->
<controls version="1.0">
    <device name="keyboard" kind="keyboard" />

    <control name="cyclic-long" source="keyboard" increase="up" decrease="down" />
    <control name="cyclic-lat" source="keyboard" increase="right" decrease="left" />
    <control name="pedals" source="keyboard" increase="d" decrease="a" />
    <control name="collective" source="keyboard" increase="w" decrease="s" />
</controls>
